    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d420">

                <pb facs="0622" n="606" xml:id="pg606"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.458/11–447: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                            Embassy in the Soviet Union
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-11-05T13:00:00-05:00">November 5, 1947—1
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1919. Urtel 3164 Nov 4 6 p. m.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d420-f1"/> Following telegram to be sent Nov 6 from President Truman
                    to President Shvernik:<anchor n="2" xml:id="d420-f2"/> “On this national
                    anniversary of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics, please convey to the
                    people of the Soviet Union the sincere greetings of the people of the United
                    States.”</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d420-f1" n="1" xml:id="d420-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed. Ambassador Smith had recommended that in view of the existing
                    situation he believed if President Truman had any intention to send an
                    anniversary message it should be limited to simple greetings. The text has been
                    reprinted in the Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>,
                    November 16, 1947, p. 960. A telegram on November 22 from Chairman Shvernik to
                    President Truman requested him “to transmit to the people of the United States
                    of America thanks for their greetings on the 30th anniversary of the founding of
                    the Soviet State.” (861.458/11–2447) The Department informed the Embassy in
                    instruction 2056 on November 13, that no member of Cabinet rank had attended the
                    reception at the Embassy of the Soviet Union in Washington on November 7; that
                    representation from the Department and the Armed Forces had been limited; and
                    the prevailing atmosphere had remained cool. (861.415/11–1347)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d420-f2" n="2" xml:id="d420-f2-content">
                <p>Nikolay Mikhailovich Shvernik was Chairman (President) of the Presidium of the
                    Supreme Council of the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
